Does that incapacitate you to make a meaningful estimate?

Let me tell you a story.  I have a friend who is a physicist as myself
and did his PhD thesis in Albuqurque.  During the defense of his thesis
he was asked to estimate, how many leaves there are in a tree (and of
course to justify his estimate).  Do you think it would have been a good
answer to say, this is not a number I spend time thinking about, it is
not important for my everyday life?  Also do you think it would have
been a wise strategy to say, well I have to work out all the details of
this, then I will write a program and maybe come up with a 1% accurate
answer a week from now?  No.  He had ten minutes to get an order of
magnitude estimate.  Being 50% off did not matter, because that is still
the right order of magnitude.  What was being examined by this question
was whether he had acquired, during his studies, the ability to
quantitatively assess completely new situations.  Now this is one thing
a physicist should be trained for (contrary to an engineer, who does not
encounter fundamentally new situations so often that he could not fall
back on a known precise routine most of the time), because even before
devising an experiment, you have to get some rough idea about whether it
will be capable to measure the often minute effect that you are looking
for. So you *must* estimate and you must think about how to get this
estimate.  It does not help just *refusing* to *think*.  And why should
I? Thinking is so much fun...

