No, it is not just guesswork.  It is reasoning.  Let's see...

How often do you think it will happen that you have 10 potions when
descending to the second level for the first time, providing you had
none in your initial inventory and no liquor shop?  I think -- in fact
know from experience -- that such a situation is very rare.  And this is
not something my flawed memory will allure me into.  I would remember,
if I found that many potions on the first level.  So, ten is too high.
Even way too high.  I would be glad to have ten potions when descending
from level 3, if I did not see any shop by then. Also, a big number of
potions is something that you notice soon in early game, as you will run
into weight management problems, not yet having a bag of holding at the
time.

Therefore, anything bigger than 3 potions per level under these
circumstances (no shops) would be a surprise.

Now one of my weight management strategies in the early game is to quaff
potions of object detection right away (if I have not yet seen all the
treasures on the level), once I keep getting burdened by picking up
stuff.  And then I check the whole level for interesting items.  From
this I know that it happens that there is no potion at all on the level
(as I was looking for a particular one more often than once).  So zero
is not absolutely rare, but more than 3 is not very frequent on levels
without shops.  If we assume that the average is 2 to 3, we may already
be on the high side, but let us assume 3 anyway.

Next, we have to figure shops in.  How many potions will that give in
addition? If there is one big shop with 25 potions, that will increase
the average by just one on 25 levels.  If there are several smaller
ones, a similar increase will ensue.  This raises the average to 4.

Everything else will change things in the fractional range.

So I *guess*, but with a reason, not just blindly, that the average
number of potions per level is not far from 4.  Whether it is above or
below, I cannot say, but I am pretty sure that the distance of the true
average from my estimate is in the range between -2 and +2, probably
closer.

Now go measure it and tell me that I am wrong :-) . Then I will start
refining my model.  For the moment it should do ;-) .


